+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Menu zoom, scroll and input priority (run #20, fixed after the console test)
+// The Logros menu is too small on a TV and its scrollbar too thin to grab
+// with the touchpad pointer, so the menu gets: a zoom (the existing
+// ImGui Scale setting), a wider scrollbar, and D-pad / stick scrolling
+// that needs no aiming.
+// * Zoom reuses the existing "ImGui Menu Scaling" setting
+//   (SohMenuSettings.cpp: "ImGuiScale", gSettings.ImGuiScale, 0 Small
+//   0.75x / 1 Normal 1.0x / 2 Large 1.5x / 3 X-Large 2.0x) and its
+//   OTRGlobals::ScaleImGui(). No new CVar, no duplicate setting. D-pad
+//   left/right steps it while the menu is open; the menu visibly resizes
+//   on each step, which is the feedback.
+// * Scroll: the scrollbar is widened to 28 px (at 1.0x scale) while the
+//   menu is open, and D-pad up/down (one notch per press, repeating while
+//   held) plus the left stick (analog) scroll the menu's section window
+//   directly (see below), so it works in every section with no aiming.
+// * Input priority (Lux's principle: the menu wins over gameplay while
+//   open): while the menu is open, game input is blocked through the
+//   ControlDeck block API, so the D-pad cannot leak into gameplay
+//   (notably ItemUnequip.cpp, which unequips items on D-pad). The menu
+//   itself is unaffected: its toggle (BACK) and the R2 pointer click go
+//   through ImGui, not ControlDeck. The block is applied once on the
+//   menu-open transition and released once on close.
+//
+// RUN #20 CONSOLE TEST — why the zoom and scroll did NOT work (root cause):
+// The first version of CheckMenuInput() returned early when the
+// "Menu Controller Navigation" setting (gControlNav) was on, on the
+// assumption that it is off by default on PS4. On Lux's console it is
+// ON, and that is PROVEN, not guessed:
+//   - the D-pad moves ImGui focus between the option boxes, which only
+//     happens when gamepad nav is active (the SDL backend only maps
+//     gamepad buttons to ImGui nav keys when NavEnableGamepad is set,
+//     and Gui::UpdateGamepadNavigation only sets it when gControlNav
+//     is non-zero);
+//   - the touchpad toggle works, and it requires gControlNav in TWO
+//     places: Gui::DrawMenu only toggles on ImGuiKey_GamepadBack when
+//     gControlNav is non-zero, and the backend only feeds that key
+//     when nav is enabled.
+// The checkbox to change it is compiled out on PS4, so Lux cannot turn
+// it off — and it MUST stay on, because the touchpad toggle depends on
+// it. So the early return skipped the zoom and scroll code entirely,
+// while nav kept owning the D-pad (focus movement, useless in Logros).
+// The scrollbar still widened because that line sits before the return.
+// The fix below does NOT touch gControlNav. Instead, while the menu is
+// open it turns ImGui gamepad nav OFF every frame (Tick runs after
+// DrawMenu in the frame — GameState_Draw runs before OnGameFrameUpdate
+// in GameState_Update — so this clear is the last write before the next
+// frame's NewFrame, and nav stays off even though DrawMenu's
+// UpdateGamepadNavigation re-sets the flag). Run #26 (Lux, "Haz que
+// llame a la funcion esc"): the menu CLOSE is no longer read from the
+// SDL controller here at all. With the menu open, a touchpad tap makes
+// Ps4MousePointer push ONE Escape key event (no BACK pulse), and
+// Gui::DrawMenu toggles the menu on ImGuiKey_Escape with no gControlNav
+// requirement — a single toggle owner, so nothing can re-open the menu
+// behind the close. The old Tick BACK close (run #25: level close +
+// guard + deferred nav restore) is deleted: Tick closed on the 4-frame
+// pulse and DrawMenu re-opened the menu on the same pulse (run #25 log:
+// 51 closes, 51 re-opens). With the menu closed a tap still starts the
+// BACK pulse as before, so the open path is unchanged.
+// The old scroll also had a second, independent flaw: it injected into
+// ImGui::GetIO().MouseWheel, which only scrolls the window UNDER THE
+// POINTER — and the pointer auto-hides after 3 s and is almost never
+// over the list. The new scroll writes the scroll target of the
+// "Main Menu" window (the outer scroller; the section content lives in
+// tall children inside it) directly, so it moves the list no matter
+// where the pointer is.
+// RUN #21 CONSOLE TEST (ps4_boot-12.log, 0 FATAL, 0 errors) — two partial
+// failures, both root-caused in the real code, both fixed below:
+//   1. Pointer vanished after zooming, never came back. NOT the pointer
+//      module: R2 clicks kept landing at new positions, so OnPadState /
+//      Update / PushMouseMotion all still worked — only the DRAWN cursor
+//      died. Cause: OTRGlobals::ScaleImGui() calls
+//      ImGui::GetStyle().ScaleAllSizes(), which does MouseCursorScale =
+//      ImTrunc(1.0 * factor); one zoom DOWN (factor 0.75 / 0.67) floors it
+//      to 0, and 0 * anything stays 0, so RenderMouseCursor draws a
+//      zero-size cursor forever. Fix: force MouseCursorScale = 1.0f every
+//      frame while the menu is open (next to the scrollbar line below).
+//   2. D-pad up/down never scrolled (stick did, zoom did). Cause: the
+//      Tick() nav clear runs once per game tick (20 Hz), but the GUI is
+//      drawn once per interpolated frame (~2x per tick at 40 fps on
+//      console), and Gui::UpdateGamepadNavigation() re-enables nav in
+//      every DrawMenu while the menu is open — so on the sub-frames after
+//      Tick's clear, NewFrame runs with nav ON and the D-pad feeds nav
+//      focus movement + ScrollToItem, which undoes the direct SetScrollY.
+//      Fix: clear NavEnableGamepad in SohMenu::DrawElement() too — the
+//      menu draw runs every sub-frame while visible, after
+//      UpdateGamepadNavigation, so nav is off at every NewFrame.
+//   What still worked in run #21 (untouched by these fixes): zoom with
+//   D-pad left/right, stick scroll, wide scrollbar, touchpad toggle open
+//   AND close, R2 click, L3+R3 combo, Hide button, live language, game
+//   input block while the menu is open, all 81 achievements.
+// RUN #22 CONSOLE TEST (ps4_boot-13.log, 0 FATAL, 0 errors) — zoom and
+// pointer FIXED, scroll still broken:
+//   Zoom works and the pointer survives zooming down: both #21 fixes
+//   confirmed on console.
+//   Scroll: neither the D-pad nor the stick moves the list anymore (in
+//   #21 the stick did). Root cause, proven from the log + the real code:
+//   CheckMenuInput() wrote SetScrollY on the "Main Menu" window, but
+//   that window is full-viewport and its content (the "Menu Block"
+//   child, ~90% of the viewport) fits, so its ScrollMax.y is 0 and the
+//   target clamps to 0 every frame. The log proves it: all 16
+//   "menu scroll dpad" lines show the target at exactly +/-180 (120 *
+//   FontGlobalScale 1.5) and never accumulating, so Scroll.y is 0 on
+//   every press. The window that actually scrolls is the section child
+//   ("<section> Settings", e.g. "Logros Settings", created in
+//   Menu::DrawElement): capped at columnHeight by size constraints
+//   while its content (the long Logros list) overflows, so it is the
+//   only "Main Menu" descendant with ScrollMax.y > 0. Fix: find that
+//   child (largest ScrollMax.y under "Main Menu/") and scroll it.
+//   Why the stick regressed #21 -> #22: in #21 the stick scrolled ONLY
+//   through Dear ImGui's own nav scrolling — NavUpdate's "Normal Manual
+//   scroll with LStick" branch calls SetScrollY on the nav window when
+//   nav is on (the SDL backend maps the stick to GamepadLStick* analog
+//   keys, imgui_impl_sdl2.cpp, and NavUpdate uses them for nav movement
+//   — so the run #21 note "the stick is not nav input" was WRONG).
+//   In #21 nav was on during most sub-frames, so ImGui scrolled the
+//   section itself; our SetScrollY on "Main Menu" was already a no-op
+//   (which is why the D-pad never scrolled: with focusable widgets
+//   present the D-pad moves focus and ScrollToItem snaps the scroll
+//   back to the focused widget near the top). In #22 the per-sub-frame
+//   nav clear in SohMenu::DrawElement() turns nav off in every
+//   sub-frame, so ImGui's own stick scroll never runs — and with our
+//   SetScrollY still aimed at the wrong window, nothing scrolls. The
+//   SetScrollY code path is identical in #21 and #22, so the stick's
+//   #21 scrolling could only have come from nav. The nav clear stays
+//   (without it the D-pad would fight the scroll through focus), the
+//   target window changes.
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Unique block id for the menu (per-caller ids; the input editor uses
+// 95237929). Fits in the uint16 the bridge takes.
+static constexpr uint16_t kMenuInputBlockId = 42424;
+static bool sMenuInputBlocked = false;
+static bool sMenuWasOpen = false;
+// BACK close guard (run #25): Tick closes the menu when BACK is down AND
+// BACK has been seen up at least once since the menu opened. The old
+// One-time boot sanity: reset a menu scale stuck at the max (see below).
+static bool sDidZoomSanity = false;
+// Repeat state for the D-pad, per direction group: frames held and the
+// current direction (reset when the direction changes, so a flip steps
+// at once instead of waiting out the old hold).
+static int sZoomHoldFrames = 0;
+static int sZoomDir = 0;
+static int sHeaderHoldFrames = 0;
+static int sHeaderDir = 0;
+static int sSectionHoldFrames = 0;
+static int sSectionDir = 0;
+// Scroll is stick-only now (run #25): the D-pad up/down moved to section
+// switching, so the scroll repeat state is gone. One diag line per scroll
+// session (stick first passing the deadzone) proves which single window
+// moves, without spamming every frame.
+static bool sWasScrolling = false;
+// Frames before the first repeat, then frames between repeats
+// (~0.4 s / ~0.15 s at 60 fps).
+static constexpr int kRepeatDelayFrames = 24;
+static constexpr int kRepeatRateFrames = 9;
+// Scroll amount (x FontGlobalScale, so it grows with the zoom): the
+// stick is analog, full deflection ≈ 480 px/s (≈ 8 px/frame at 60 fps).
+static constexpr float kScrollStickPxPerFrame = 8.0f;
+
+static bool RepeatStep(int dir, int& holdFrames, int& lastDir) {
+    if (dir == 0) {
+        holdFrames = 0;
+        lastDir = 0;
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (dir != lastDir) {
+        holdFrames = 0;
+        lastDir = dir;
+    }
+    ++holdFrames;
+    return holdFrames == 1 || (holdFrames > kRepeatDelayFrames &&
+                               (holdFrames - kRepeatDelayFrames) % kRepeatRateFrames == 0);
+}
+
+// The ONE menu window that should scroll (run #25, Lux's rule: the
+// section under the cursor scrolls — and ONLY that one).
+//
+// Run #22 proved the old target ("Main Menu") never scrolls: it is
+// full-viewport and its content fits, so its ScrollMax.y is 0 and
+// SetScrollY clamps to 0 (ps4_boot-13.log: Scroll.y stuck at 0 on every
+// D-pad press). The scrollable window is the section child ("<section>
+// Settings", e.g. "Logros Settings", created in Menu::DrawElement):
+// capped at columnHeight by size constraints while its content overflows,
+// so it is the only descendant of "Main Menu" with ScrollMax.y > 0.
+// Child windows are named "Parent/Child_<id>" (imgui.cpp v1.91.9b,
+// BeginChildEx), so match the "Main Menu/" prefix plus " Settings".
+//
+// Which section scrolls (Lux, 2026-11-19): the one UNDER THE CURSOR.
+// The hovered window (ImGuiContext::HoveredWindow, from the last
+// NewFrame's hover test) names it; if the pointer is over a section
+// child, that section scrolls. If the pointer is over anything else
+// (the sidebar, the header, the menu background, or outside the menu —
+// e.g. hidden after 3 s idle over a non-section area), fall back to the
+// single column of the active section with the largest ScrollMax.y.
+//
+// Run #24 returned ALL sibling columns of the section and moved them
+// together — that is exactly what Lux does NOT want (run #24 log: each
+// press moved all 3 Minigames columns and both Shuffles columns). Now
+// ONE window is returned, never several. Windows only count while
+// Active (drawn this frame); Tick() runs after DrawMenu in the frame,
+// so the values are from the draw that just happened.
+static ImGuiWindow* FindMenuScrollWindow() {
+    ImGuiContext* ctx = ImGui::GetCurrentContext();
+    if (ctx == nullptr) {
+        return nullptr;
+    }
+    // The hovered section window, if any.
+    ImGuiWindow* hovered = ctx->HoveredWindow;
+    if (hovered != nullptr && hovered->Active && hovered->Name != nullptr &&
+        strncmp(hovered->Name, "Main Menu/", 10) == 0 && strstr(hovered->Name, " Settings") != nullptr &&
+        hovered->ScrollMax.y > 0.0f) {
+        return hovered;
+    }
+    // Fallback: the single section column with the largest ScrollMax.
+    ImGuiWindow* best = nullptr;
+    for (ImGuiWindow* window : ctx->Windows) {
+        if (window == nullptr || !window->Active) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (window->Name == nullptr || strncmp(window->Name, "Main Menu/", 10) != 0 ||
+            strstr(window->Name, " Settings") == nullptr) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (window->ScrollMax.y <= 0.0f) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (best == nullptr || window->ScrollMax.y > best->ScrollMax.y) {
+            best = window;
+        }
+    }
+    return best;
+}
+
+// Close the menu from code (run #26: only the L3+R3 quick restart uses
+// this now). The touchpad close is owned by DrawMenu via the injected
+// Escape key (Ps4MousePointer, libultraship) — Tick no longer closes the
+// menu, so there is no second toggle owner to re-open it (run #25 log:
+// the 4-frame BACK pulse re-opened the menu 51/51 times). Force closed,
+// never toggle: ToggleVisibility would OPEN a closed menu if called
+// blind, and this must only ever close. No nav restore here either: the
+// deferred restore (run #25) is gone with the BACK close — its stated
+// purpose (re-arm the next BACK open) cannot be the mechanism anyway,
+// because the next DrawMenu clears nav while the menu is closed, and run
+// #24 proves opens work without any deferred restore (see
+// run26-progress.md). If the #26 console test shows the open broke, the
+// fix is a real nav re-arm, not this restore.
+static void CloseMenuNow() {
+    auto gui = Ship::Context::GetRawInstance()->GetWindow()->GetGui();
+    if (gui == nullptr) {
+        return;
+    }
+    auto menu = gui->GetMenu();
+    if (menu == nullptr) {
+        return;
+    }
+    menu->Hide(); // public force-close (GuiElement::Hide == SetVisibility(false),
+                  // which GuiWindow overrides to also sync the visibility CVar).
+                  // SetVisibility itself is protected, so Hide() is the way in.
+    if (sMenuInputBlocked) {
+        ControllerUnblockGameInput(kMenuInputBlockId);
+        sMenuInputBlocked = false;
+    }
+    sMenuWasOpen = false;
+    sZoomHoldFrames = 0;
+    sZoomDir = 0;
+    sHeaderHoldFrames = 0;
+    sHeaderDir = 0;
+    sSectionHoldFrames = 0;
+    sSectionDir = 0;
+    sWasScrolling = false;
+}
+
+static void CheckMenuInput() {
+    // One-time boot sanity: if the menu scale is stuck at the max
+    // (X-Large, 2.0x), Lux cannot see the options and the D-pad zoom is
+    // the way back — never boot into an unusable menu. Only the max is
+    // reset; 1.5x is a legitimate choice and is left alone. He can set
+    // it big again from Settings -> General -> ImGui Menu Scaling (and
+    // the D-pad zoom below now works to change it back).
+    if (!sDidZoomSanity) {
+        sDidZoomSanity = true;
+        if (CVarGetInteger(CVAR_SETTING("ImGuiScale"), 1) == 3) {
+            CVarSetInteger(CVAR_SETTING("ImGuiScale"), 1);
+            OTRGlobals::Instance->ScaleImGui();
+        }
+    }
+
+    bool menuOpen = IsMenuOpen();
+
+    // --- input priority: block game input while the menu is open ---
+    if (menuOpen && !sMenuWasOpen) {
+        ControllerBlockGameInput(kMenuInputBlockId);
+        sMenuInputBlocked = true;
+    } else if (!menuOpen && sMenuWasOpen && sMenuInputBlocked) {
+        ControllerUnblockGameInput(kMenuInputBlockId);
+        sMenuInputBlocked = false;
+    }
+    sMenuWasOpen = menuOpen;
+
+    if (!menuOpen) {
+        sZoomHoldFrames = 0;
+        sZoomDir = 0;
+        sHeaderHoldFrames = 0;
+        sHeaderDir = 0;
+        sSectionHoldFrames = 0;
+        sSectionDir = 0;
+        sWasScrolling = false;
+        return;
+    }
+
+    // --- gamepad nav OFF while the menu is open: the D-pad and the
+    // sticks are ours now, not ImGui's focus movement. (See the header
+    // comment: this runs after DrawMenu in the frame, so the clear wins
+    // over UpdateGamepadNavigation for the next frame's NewFrame.) ---
+    ImGui::GetIO().ConfigFlags &= ~ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableGamepad;
+
+    // --- wider scrollbar while the menu is open ---
+    // Set every frame: ScaleImGui()'s ScaleAllSizes rescales it on zoom
+    // change, and this keeps it at 28 px * the current font scale.
+    ImGui::GetStyle().ScrollbarSize = 28.0f * ImGui::GetIO().FontGlobalScale;
+
+    // --- cursor scale: ScaleImGui()'s ScaleAllSizes truncates it ---
+    // Run #21 console test: the pointer vanished after zooming and never
+    // came back, even though the pointer module kept tracking (R2 clicks
+    // still landed at new positions). Cause: ScaleAllSizes does
+    // MouseCursorScale = ImTrunc(1.0 * factor), so one zoom DOWN (2.0x ->
+    // 1.5x, factor 0.75; 1.5x -> 1.0x, factor 0.67) floors it to 0 — and
+    // 0 * anything stays 0, so RenderMouseCursor draws a zero-size cursor
+    // forever (imgui.cpp v1.91.9b: RenderMouseCursor scales by
+    // g.Style.MouseCursorScale). Force it back every frame, like the
+    // scrollbar above; the cursor is only drawn while the menu is open.
+    ImGui::GetStyle().MouseCursorScale = 1.0f;
+
+    // --- NO BACK close here anymore (run #26, Lux) ---
+    // The touchpad close is owned by Gui::DrawMenu via the injected
+    // Escape key (Ps4MousePointer, libultraship): with the menu open a
+    // tap pushes ONE SDL_KEYDOWN/KEYUP pair for Escape and NO BACK pulse,
+    // so DrawMenu toggles the menu exactly once and nothing can re-open
+    // it behind the close. The old Tick BACK close (level close + guard +
+    // deferred nav restore, run #25) is deleted: Tick closed on the
+    // 4-frame pulse and DrawMenu then re-opened the menu on the same
+    // pulse (run #25 log: 51/51). With the menu closed a tap still
+    // starts the BACK pulse as before, so the open path is unchanged.
+
+    // --- zoom: D-pad left/right steps the existing ImGuiScale setting ---
+    const int zoomDir = (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_DPAD_RIGHT) ? 1 : 0) -
+                        (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_DPAD_LEFT) ? 1 : 0);
+    if (RepeatStep(zoomDir, sZoomHoldFrames, sZoomDir)) {
+        const int current = CVarGetInteger(CVAR_SETTING("ImGuiScale"), 1);
+        int next = current + zoomDir;
+        if (next < 0) {
+            next = 0;
+        }
+        if (next > 3) {
+            next = 3;
+        }
+        if (next != current) {
+            CVarSetInteger(CVAR_SETTING("ImGuiScale"), next);
+            OTRGlobals::Instance->ScaleImGui();
+            // TEMP DIAG: prove the zoom step (and direction) on console.
+            SPDLOG_INFO("[PS4] [diag] menu zoom -> scale {} (dir {})", next, zoomDir);
+            spdlog::default_logger()->flush();
+        }
+    }
+
+    // --- header switching: R1 = next header, L1 = previous (run #25) ---
+    // Lux: R1/L1 switch the TOP headers (Logros, Network, ...), not the
+    // sub-sections. CycleHeader sets only the ActiveHeader CVar; the
+    // section resolves from that header's sidebar CVar — its last
+    // visited section, or its first section when the stored value is
+    // invalid — exactly like tapping the header tab (Menu::DrawElement
+    // falls back to sidebarOrder.at(0)). Same input path as zoom (SDL
+    // controller via Tick, with hold-repeat). Game input is blocked
+    // while the menu is open, so R1/L1 cannot reach the game.
+    const int headerDir = (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_RIGHTSHOULDER) ? 1 : 0) -
+                          (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_LEFTSHOULDER) ? 1 : 0);
+    if (RepeatStep(headerDir, sHeaderHoldFrames, sHeaderDir)) {
+        auto gui = Ship::Context::GetRawInstance()->GetWindow()->GetGui();
+        if (gui != nullptr) {
+            auto menu = std::static_pointer_cast<Ship::Menu>(gui->GetMenu());
+            if (menu != nullptr) {
+                menu->CycleHeader(headerDir);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    // --- section switching: D-pad up/down (run #25, direction fixed run #26) ---
+    // Lux: the D-pad up/down now does what R1/L1 did — cycle the flat
+    // (header, section) list, so down from the last section of one
+    // header lands on the first section of the next header. Same input
+    // path (SDL controller via Tick, with hold-repeat).
+    // Run #26: the direction was INVERTED (Lux, run #25 console test:
+    // up moved the selection down). CycleSidebarSection does
+    // next = (current + dir) % total, so dir +1 = the NEXT section =
+    // visually DOWN the list. D-pad UP must therefore be -1 and DOWN +1.
+    const int sectionDir = (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_DPAD_DOWN) ? 1 : 0) -
+                           (PadButton(SDL_CONTROLLER_BUTTON_DPAD_UP) ? 1 : 0);
+    if (RepeatStep(sectionDir, sSectionHoldFrames, sSectionDir)) {
+        auto gui = Ship::Context::GetRawInstance()->GetWindow()->GetGui();
+        if (gui != nullptr) {
+            auto menu = std::static_pointer_cast<Ship::Menu>(gui->GetMenu());
+            if (menu != nullptr) {
+                menu->CycleSidebarSection(sectionDir);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    // --- scroll: left stick only (run #25) ---
+    // The D-pad up/down moved to section switching, so the stick is now
+    // the ONLY scroll input. Applied DIRECTLY to the ONE menu section
+    // window that scrolls (see FindMenuScrollWindow: the section under
+    // the cursor, else the single largest-ScrollMax column of the active
+    // section — never several), instead of injecting into
+    // io.MouseWheel — the wheel only scrolls the window UNDER THE
+    // POINTER, and the pointer auto-hides and is almost never over the
+    // list. Up scrolls up (Scroll.y decreases). Nav stays off while the
+    // menu is open (see above), so no focus move / ScrollToItem fights
+    // this: ImGui's own stick scroll is also off, which is why the stick
+    // needs this path too.
+    float scrollDelta = 0.0f;
+    const float fontScale = ImGui::GetIO().FontGlobalScale;
+    // Left stick Y: up is negative on the axis. Deadzone ~8000/32768.
+    const int16_t stickY = PadAxis(SDL_CONTROLLER_AXIS_LEFTY);
+    if (stickY < -8000 || stickY > 8000) {
+        const float deflection = static_cast<float>(-stickY) / 32768.0f;
+        scrollDelta -= deflection * kScrollStickPxPerFrame * fontScale;
+    }
+    if (scrollDelta != 0.0f) {
+        ImGuiWindow* scrollWindow = FindMenuScrollWindow();
+        if (scrollWindow != nullptr) {
+            // Run #23 bug 1: while R2 is held on this window's scrollbar,
+            // ScrollbarEx re-derives Scroll.y from the (static) mouse
+            // position every frame and overwrites the SetScrollY below —
+            // the scroll looks slower / stuck while R2 is held. Release
+            // that drag when we scroll the same window with the stick:
+            // the stick input wins over the accidental scrollbar hold.
+            // The scrollbar is the active widget exactly while held
+            // (imgui_widgets.cpp v1.91.9b: SetActiveID on grab).
+            if (ImGui::GetActiveID() == ImGui::GetWindowScrollbarID(scrollWindow, ImGuiAxis_Y)) {
+                ImGui::ClearActiveID();
+            }
+            // TEMP DIAG: first frame of a scroll session only (the
+            // stick scrolls every frame and would spam). Logs the single
+            // window that moves, so the next console log proves only
+            // one window moves per gesture: Scroll.y must grow across
+            // gestures and no second window may move on the same one.
+            // (SetScrollY only sets ScrollTarget — Scroll.y itself does
+            // not change until the next frame — so the values below are
+            // the pre-scroll position and the target.)
+            if (!sWasScrolling) {
+                SPDLOG_INFO("[PS4] [diag] menu scroll stick win {} Scroll.y {:.0f}/{:.0f} -> target {:.0f}",
+                            scrollWindow->Name, scrollWindow->Scroll.y, scrollWindow->ScrollMax.y,
+                            scrollWindow->Scroll.y + scrollDelta);
+                spdlog::default_logger()->flush();
+            }
+            ImGui::SetScrollY(scrollWindow, scrollWindow->Scroll.y + scrollDelta);
+        }
+    }
+    sWasScrolling = (scrollDelta != 0.0f);
+}
+
+// Run #30: completion fanfare deferred past the credits. If the 112th
+// unlock lands in END_CREDITS (e.g. #31), the fanfare would stomp the
+// credits music; latch it and play it on the first normal gameplay frame.
+static bool sFanfarePending = false;
+
+static void Tick() {
+    // Test-toast combo and menu input poll every frame (the unlock check
+    // below is throttled). The fight tracker also polls every frame: its
+    // signals are transient (a one-frame lens flash, a healthAccumulator
+    // drain) and the 30-frame throttle would miss them.
+    CheckL3R3Combo();
+    CheckMenuInput();
+    UpdateFightTracker();
+    // Run #29: the pickup tracker (1007/1013) and the Boss Rush damage
+    // poll (1021) are also per-frame — their signals are transient (the
+    // player can warp out of the source scene before the next throttle
+    // tick, and damage is a one-frame health drop).
+    UpdateRandoPickupTracker();
+    UpdateBossRushDamage();
+    // Run #30: deferred completion fanfare (see sFanfarePending above).
+    if (sFanfarePending && gSaveContext.gameMode == GAMEMODE_NORMAL && GameInteractor::IsSaveLoaded()) {
+        sFanfarePending = false;
+        if (RewardEnabled("Fanfare")) {
+            Audio_PlayFanfare(NA_BGM_MEDALLION_GET | 0x900);
+        }
+    }
+    // Throttle: check every 30 frames. The logic runs at 20 Hz (not 60
+    // fps — R_UPDATE_RATE = 3), so 30 frames is ~1.5 s. Achievements are
+    // meta-progression; a 1.5 s delay is unnoticeable.
+    static int frameCounter = 0;
+    if ((++frameCounter % 30) != 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+
+    const int unlockedBefore = UnlockedCount();
+    std::vector<const Def*> newlyUnlocked;
+    auto unlock = [&newlyUnlocked](const Def* def) {
+        CVarSetInteger(UnlockedCVar(def->id).c_str(), 1);
+        newlyUnlocked.push_back(def);
+        // Consume the runtime hook latch (if any) so a fired hook cannot
+        // re-trigger after its CVar is set.
+        if (def->id > 0 && def->id < 1024) {
+            sHookFired[def->id] = false;
+        }
+        SPDLOG_INFO("[Achievements] Unlocked {} ({} pts)", def->titleEn, def->points);
+    };
+
+    // #31 Home Sweet Home (win condition) is checked BEFORE the
+    // save-loaded gate: there is no persistent "game beaten" flag in the
+    // save; the only signal is the live gameMode END_CREDITS during the
+    // ending (z_demo.c sets it in the two ending cutscene terminators and
+    // nowhere else). IsSaveLoaded() is false during the credits (it
+    // requires GAMEMODE_NORMAL), so the gated loop below would never see
+    // it. The unlock latches to its CVar + SaveNow, so it persists. The
+    // gameMode check needs no extra guard: END_CREDITS is only reachable
+    // by beating Ganon, and the demo system resets it to NORMAL when the
+    // credits end.
+    const Def* winDef = FindDef(31);
+    if (winDef != nullptr && winDef->implemented && !IsUnlocked(31) &&
+        gSaveContext.gameMode == GAMEMODE_END_CREDITS) {
+        unlock(winDef);
+    }
+
+    // Randomizer win achievements (1002, 1008, 1010, 1011, 1012, 1020):
+    // same END_CREDITS signal as #31, gated on a seed being loaded. The
+    // timestamps inside CheckRandoWin() tell a Ganon win apart from a
+    // Triforce Hunt win. #1022 (Five Seeds) is counted here too: the
+    // defeat timestamp is the key, so the 1.5 s re-runs during the
+    // credits cannot count one win twice (see CountRandoWin).
+    if (IS_RANDO && gSaveContext.gameMode == GAMEMODE_END_CREDITS) {
+        CountRandoWin();
+        for (int id : { 1002, 1008, 1010, 1011, 1012, 1020 }) {
+            const Def* def = FindDef(id);
+            if (def != nullptr && def->implemented && !IsUnlocked(id) && CheckRandoWin(id)) {
+                unlock(def);
+            }
+        }
+        // #1022 Five Seeds: unlock as soon as the counter reaches 5.
+        const Def* fiveSeeds = FindDef(1022);
+        if (fiveSeeds != nullptr && fiveSeeds->implemented && !IsUnlocked(1022) &&
+            CVarGetInteger(kSeedsCompleted, 0) >= 5) {
+            unlock(fiveSeeds);
+        }
+    }
+
+    // Everything else: only while a save is loaded (title screen / file
+    // select have no save).
+    if (GameInteractor::IsSaveLoaded()) {
+        for (const Def& def : kDefs) {
+            if (!def.implemented || IsEndCreditsWin(def.id) || IsUnlocked(def.id)) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            if (CheckCondition(def.id)) {
+                unlock(&def);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    if (newlyUnlocked.empty()) {
+        return;
+    }
+    CVarSetInteger(kHasUnseen, 1);
+    SaveNow();
+    EmitUnlockToast(newlyUnlocked, unlockedBefore == 0);
+    // Run #30: unlock jingle + completion fanfare. One jingle per tick
+    // even if several unlocked at once (e.g. loading an old save).
+    // NOTE: there is no Audio_PlaySfx(u16) in this tree; the simple
+    // centered-SFX call is the equivalent (functions.h:984).
+    if (RewardEnabled("Jingle")) {
+        Sfx_PlaySfxCentered(NA_SE_SY_CORRECT_CHIME);
+    }
+    // Fanfare exactly once, when the total first reaches 112 (all
+    // implementable achievements). In END_CREDITS it is deferred (see
+    // sFanfarePending): the credits music must not be stomped.
+    if (unlockedBefore < 112 && UnlockedCount() >= 112 && RewardEnabled("Fanfare")) {
+        if (gSaveContext.gameMode == GAMEMODE_END_CREDITS) {
+            sFanfarePending = true;
+        } else {
+            Audio_PlayFanfare(NA_BGM_MEDALLION_GET | 0x900);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Init
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+void Init() {
+    // A toast can outlive the session (game closed while it was up), leaving
+    // a stale 1 in the config; no toast is visible at boot.
+    CVarSetInteger(kToastVisible, 0);
+    GameInteractor::Instance->RegisterGameHook<GameInteractor::OnGameFrameUpdate>(Tick);
+    RegisterFightHooks();
+}
+
+static RegisterShipInitFunc initFunc(Init);
+
+} // namespace Achievements
diff --git a/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/Achievements.h b/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/Achievements.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..50a32dad
--- /dev/null
+++ b/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/Achievements.h
@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <imgui.h>
+#include <string>
+#include <vector>
+
+// Local achievement system for Ship of Harkinian (PS4 port, 9.3.0).
+//
+// Design (closed 2026-11-12): 113 local achievements, 1005 points
+// (90 vanilla / 625 pts + 23 Randomizer / 380 pts). Local only: NOT PSN
+// trophies, NOT synced to RetroAchievements. Meta-progression: unlocks
+// persist across saves and seeds and give no gameplay advantage.
+// Implemented (run #29): 112/113, 1000/1005 pts — 89 vanilla / 620 pts
+// + 23 Randomizer / 380 pts. #46 (Walking on a Tightrope, 5 pts) stays
+// unimplemented by Lux's decision. Each achievement carries title +
+// description in EN and ES; the display language is chosen in the
+// Logros menu (gAchievements.Language).
+// The 14 story achievements (13 progression + 1 win condition) are hidden
+// ("???") until unlocked, and can be revealed by tapping them (spoiler
+// warning). Visual rewards (slot, titles, Triforce, logo, HUD, secret
+// message, jingle, fanfare) landed in run #30: menu header + reward cards
+// + icons (SohMenuAchievements.cpp), logo glow/gold (z_en_mag.c), HUD
+// Triforce icon (z_parameter.c), jingle/fanfare (Tick, below). Still
+// pending: the slot tint on the file-select screen (z_file_choose.c) —
+// SlotColor() below is ready for it.
+
+namespace Achievements {
+
+enum class Category { Vanilla, Randomizer };
+
+enum class Type { Progression, WinCondition, Missable, None };
+
+struct Def {
+    int id;               // Vanilla: RetroAchievements number (1-100, gaps = cut).
+                          // Randomizer: 1000 + number (1001-1022).
+    const char* titleEn;  // English title
+    const char* titleEs;  // Spanish title
+    const char* descEn;   // English description
+    const char* descEs;   // Spanish description
+    int points;
+    Category category;
+    Type type;
+    bool hidden;      // Story achievement: shown as "???" until unlocked.
+    const char* icon; // OTR texture path (e.g. "__OTR__textures/...") or nullptr.
+    bool implemented; // Detection wired (phase 1: only the 13 progression).
+};
+
+// Full registry (all 113 entries). Detection is wired only for the
+// implemented ones; the rest show as locked in the Logros menu.
+const std::vector<Def>& GetDefs();
+const Def* FindDef(int id);
+
+bool IsUnlocked(int id);
+bool IsRevealed(int id);
+void Reveal(int id); // Mark a hidden achievement as revealed (spoiler shown).
+void Hide(int id);   // Hide a revealed-but-still-locked story achievement again
+                     // (clears its Revealed CVar; the row shows "???" again).
+                     // Unlocked achievements are never hidden.
+bool HasUnseen();    // True when something new unlocked since the Logros menu
+                     // was last opened (drives the badge on the Logros tab).
+void MarkSeen();     // Clear the unseen flag (called when the Logros menu opens).
+
+// Destructive reset (run #26, Lux: "Reiniciar logros" menu option, with
+// confirmation): clears ALL unlocks, ALL reveals, the unseen flag and
+// the bribe counter (gAchievements.BribeCount). Language and reward
+// toggles are untouched. The registry does not change.
+void ResetAll();
+
+// Display language: 0 = English, 1 = Español (CVar gAchievements.Language).
+int Language();
+bool IsSpanish();
+std::string Title(const Def& def);
+std::string Desc(const Def& def);
+// Translate a menu UI string to the display language.
+std::string Tr(const std::string& en, const std::string& es);
+
+int TotalPoints();    // 1000 (625 vanilla + 375 randomizer).
+int UnlockedCount();  // Unlocked achievements across both categories.
+int UnlockedPoints(); // Sum of points of unlocked achievements.
+int CategoryPoints(Category category);
+int CategoryUnlockedPoints(Category category);
+int CategoryUnlockedCount(Category category);
+int CategoryTotalCount(Category category);
+
+// Reward toggles (run #30: the visuals are live; every reward can be
+// turned off from the Logros menu). Each reward has its own switch in
+// the Logros menu; SecretMessage is the only one OFF by default.
+bool RewardEnabled(const char* rewardCvarSuffix);
+
+// Ranks, titles and slot (run #30, visual rewards). Two independent
+// axes: titles by COUNT of unlocked achievements, slot division by
+// POINTS. "Complete" = 112 unlocked (all implementable achievements;
+// #46 cannot unlock, so 113 is unreachable). Rank/title names are
+// English proper nouns in both display languages.
+struct RankInfo {
+    const char* name; // Iron, Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum, Diamond, Ascendant, Immortal, Radiant
+    ImVec4 color;     // mockup colour for this rank (Radiant: #e6c619)
+    int division;     // 1-25 (40 pts each); 25 = Radiant at 1000 pts
+    int pointsToNext; // points missing for the next division, -1 at Radiant
+};
+RankInfo RankForPoints(int points);
+RankInfo CurrentRank(); // RankForPoints(UnlockedPoints())
+const char* TitleForCount(int count); // Iron 0-4, Bronze 5, Silver 10, Gold 15, Platinum 20,
+// Diamond 25, Ascendant 50, Immortal 75, Radiant 112
+// Next title after `count` (nullptr at Radiant); `needed` gets how many
+// more unlocks it takes (0 at Radiant).
+const char* NextTitleForCount(int count, int* needed);
+int TriforcePieces(); // 0-3: one piece at 25, 50 and 75 unlocks
+bool IsComplete();    // UnlockedCount() >= 112
+// Slot colour: the current rank colour, or the free colour picked in
+// the Logros menu (gAchievements.SlotColorR/G/B, default Radiant gold)
+// once 1000 pts are reached. White when the Slot reward is off.
+ImVec4 SlotColor();
+
+// Navigate the SoH menu to the Logros section (used by the toast action).
+void OpenLogrosMenu();
+
+// Called once at boot (via RegisterShipInitFunc): registers the frame hook.
+void Init();
+
+} // namespace Achievements
diff --git a/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/AchievementsNotify.h b/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/AchievementsNotify.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..8e791821
--- /dev/null
+++ b/soh/soh/Enhancements/Achievements/AchievementsNotify.h
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+#pragma once
+// Bridge so C actor code can report an achievement event to the C++
+// Achievements module. The call only latches a pending flag; Tick()
+// performs the actual unlock + toast on its normal path, so all unlock
+// logic stays in one place.
+//
+// Hook points (run #23, HOOK achievements):
+//   #35 Money Solves Everything — z_en_heishi2.c, after the -10 rupee bribe.
+//   #38 You Make Me Feel So Good — z_en_cow.c, when the player takes the milk.
+//   #90 Accomplished Angler — z_fishing.c, when the player keeps a Hyrule Loach.
+//   #1023 Money Solves Everything, Twenty Times — z_en_heishi2.c, same bribe
+//     call site; counts bribes paid in Randomizer seeds toward the 20-bribe
+//     total (see Achievements_NotifyBribe in Achievements.cpp).
+//
+// Hook points (run #29, DIFÍCIL + Boss Rush):
+//   #1 Spider Net — z_boss_goma.c, when Gohma starts climbing the wall
+//     back to the ceiling (BossGoma_SetupWallClimb).
+//   #13 Drunken Master / #95 Whac-A-Mole / #98 Double Trouble — the boss
+//     actors report every hit that actually reduces their health, with
+//     the toucher's dmgFlags (BossGanondrof_CollisionCheck, 2 sites;
+//     BossFd2_CollisionCheck, 2 sites; the merged Twinrova's stunned
+//     branch). See Achievements_NotifyBossHit in Achievements.cpp.
+//   #1021 Flawless Boss Rush — BossRush.cpp, when a new Boss Rush file is
+//     created (BossRush_InitSave); resets the damage flag for the attempt.
+//
+// Read-only getters (run #30, visual rewards): C game code (title logo,
+// HUD icon) reads the unlock state through these — same bridge pattern,
+// implemented in Achievements.cpp over UnlockedCount()/UnlockedPoints()/
+// RewardEnabled(). The visuals turn off on their own after ResetAll()
+// because the getters read the live CVars.
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+void Achievements_NotifyHook(int achievementId);
+void Achievements_NotifyBribe(void);
+void Achievements_NotifyBossHit(unsigned int dmgFlags); // u32; spelled out so the header compiles in C++ before the C headers define u32
+void Achievements_NotifyGohmaRetreat(void);
+void Achievements_NotifyBossRushStart(void);
+int Achievements_GetUnlockedCount(void);
+int Achievements_GetUnlockedPoints(void);
+int Achievements_IsRewardEnabled(const char* suffix); // 1 = on (RewardEnabled); "Slot", "LogoGlow", "HudIcon", ...
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
